diagnosis_text:
After exhaustive inspection of the control plane, data plane, and link layer across all five routers and both hosts, the network is currently healthy. ICMP reachability between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is confirmed in both directions with zero packet loss, and traceroutes verify the complete end-to-end forwarding path: pc_0_0 → leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_0 → leaf_router_0_1 → pc_0_1. All eight BGP sessions are in Established state, all link interfaces are UP, and all routing tables carry the correct prefixes. The fault the user experienced was almost certainly transient, caused by BGP session disruption during the maintenance window itself (e.g., a router restart or configuration reload reset the sessions), which briefly withdrew inter-rack routes and caused the "everything times out" symptom. The network reconverged on its own once the maintenance activity completed. There is one latent resilience concern worth noting: no router in the fabric has `maximum-paths` (ECMP) configured, so BGP best-path selection concentrates all active forwarding on spine_router_0_0 (which wins due to its lower BGP router-ID, 172.16.0.1, over spine_router_0_1's 172.16.0.3). spine_router_0_1 exists only as a cold standby. A future failure of spine_router_0_0 will again cause a connectivity gap lasting BGP hold-timer/reconvergence time. The corrective fix is to add `maximum-paths ebgp 2` under each leaf router's BGP address-family, enabling ECMP across both spines so traffic is distributed and resilient without any single-spine dependency.
=== END ===